WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.140
Stride convolutions
is another piece of

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.850
the basic building block of

3
00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.375
convolutions as using
convolution neural networks.

4
00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.010
Let me show you an example.

5
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.890
Let's say you want to
convolve this serves by

6
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.740
seven image with this
three by three filter.

7
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.245
Except that instead of
doing the usual way,

8
00:00:19.245 --> 00:00:23.325
we're going to do it
with a stride of two.

9
00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:28.080
What that means, is you take
the element wise product as

10
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.880
usual in this upper
left three by

11
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.805
three region and then
multiply and add,

12
00:00:32.805 --> 00:00:35.415
and that gives you 91.

13
00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:37.140
But then instead of stepping

14
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.985
the blue box over by one step,

15
00:00:38.985 --> 00:00:42.135
we're going to step
it over by two steps.

16
00:00:42.135 --> 00:00:46.220
We're going to make it hop
over two steps like so.

17
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.620
Notice how the upper
left-hand corner has

18
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.050
gone from this start
to this start,

19
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.910
jumping over one position.

20
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:56.240
Then you do the usual
element-wise product and summing,

21
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.115
that gives you tones of 100.

22
00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:00.570
Now we're going
to do that again,

23
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.180
and make the blue box
jump over by two steps.

24
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.930
You end up there and
that gives you 83.

25
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.135
Now when you go to the next row,

26
00:01:11.135 --> 00:01:14.925
you again actually take two
steps instead of one step.

27
00:01:14.925 --> 00:01:17.870
We're going to move the
blue box over there.

28
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:23.145
Notice how we're skipping
over one of the positions.

29
00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:25.065
Then just gives you 69,

30
00:01:25.065 --> 00:01:27.675
and now you gain
step over two steps.

31
00:01:27.675 --> 00:01:30.525
This gives you 91, and so on.

32
00:01:30.525 --> 00:01:31.695
So 127.

33
00:01:31.695 --> 00:01:33.870
Then for the final row,

34
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:38.680
44, 72, and 74.

35
00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:42.170
In this example, we convolve

36
00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.240
with a seven by
seven matrix of a,

37
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.605
three by three matrix,

38
00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:49.225
and we get a, three
by three output.

39
00:01:49.225 --> 00:01:51.650
The input and output
dimensions turns

40
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.975
out to be governed by
the following formula.

41
00:01:53.975 --> 00:01:57.020
If you have an n by
n image convolve

42
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.170
with an f by f filter.

43
00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.320
If you use adding,

44
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:09.810
p and stride s. In this example,

45
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.130
s is equal to two.

46
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:16.890
Then you end up with an
output that is n plus 2p,

47
00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.990
minus f. Now because you're

48
00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.260
stepping s steps at

49
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.705
a time this up just
one step at a time,

50
00:02:22.705 --> 00:02:26.065
you know divide by s plus 1,

51
00:02:26.065 --> 00:02:29.060
and then by the same thing.

52
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.070
In our example we have,

53
00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:40.605
7+0-3 divided by 2,

54
00:02:40.605 --> 00:02:45.300
that's a stride plus
one equals, let's see,

55
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.710
that's 4/2+1=3,

56
00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.200
which is why we wind up with
this three by three output.

57
00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.660
Now, just one last detail,

58
00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.330
which is one of this
fraction is not an integer.

59
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:05.540
In that case, we're going
to round this down.

60
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:09.275
This notation denotes
the four or something.

61
00:03:09.275 --> 00:03:14.550
This is also called
the floor of z.

62
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:15.840
It means taking z,

63
00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.150
and rounding down to
the nearest integer.

64
00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.600
If the way this
is implemented is

65
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.610
that you take this type of

66
00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.430
blue box multiplication only
if the blue box is fully

67
00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:29.225
contained within the image or
the image plus the padding.

68
00:03:29.225 --> 00:03:31.265
If any of this blue box,

69
00:03:31.265 --> 00:03:33.560
part of it hangs
outside then you

70
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.915
just do not do that computation.

71
00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:38.510
Then it turns out that if

72
00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.940
that's a convention
that your feedback,

73
00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.450
the filter must lie entirely
within your image or

74
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.445
the image plus the
padding region

75
00:03:46.445 --> 00:03:48.860
before there's a corresponding
output generator.

76
00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.530
That's convention. Then
the right thing to do,

77
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:57.005
to compute the output
dimension is to round down,

78
00:03:57.005 --> 00:04:01.675
in case this n+2p-f/s
is not an integer.

79
00:04:01.675 --> 00:04:04.200
Just to summarize
the dimensions,

80
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.100
if you have an n by n
matrix or n by n image,

81
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.570
that you convolve with an
f by f matrix and if I

82
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.270
filter with padding
p and stride s,

83
00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.620
then the output size,

84
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.120
will have this dimension.

85
00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.570
It is nice we can choose
all of these numbers,

86
00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.010
so that isn't integer,

87
00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.470
although sometimes
you don't have to do

88
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.290
that and rounding down
this is just fine as well.

89
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.610
But please feel free
to work through

90
00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.760
a few examples of
values of n, f, p,

91
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.520
and s for yourself to convince
yourself if you want,

92
00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.265
that this formula is correct
for the output size.

93
00:04:41.265 --> 00:04:43.225
Now, before moving on,

94
00:04:43.225 --> 00:04:45.550
there is a technical
comment I want to make

95
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:48.660
about cross-correlation
verses convolutions.

96
00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.860
This won't affect
what you have to

97
00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.740
do to implement convolution
neural networks.

98
00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.505
But depending on the DVD,

99
00:04:56.505 --> 00:04:59.765
different math textbook or
signal processing textbook,

100
00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:01.730
there is one other

101
00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.910
possible inconsistency
in the notation.

102
00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.220
Which is that if you look at

103
00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.120
a typical math textbook the way

104
00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.130
that the convolution is defined,

105
00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.520
before doing the element-wise
product and summing,

106
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.005
there's actually one other step

107
00:05:15.005 --> 00:05:16.115
that you would first take,

108
00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.980
which is to convolve
this six by six matrix,

109
00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.435
and this three by three filter,

110
00:05:20.435 --> 00:05:23.030
you first take the
three by three filter,

111
00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.520
and flip it on the

112
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.625
horizontal as well as
the vertical axis.

113
00:05:28.625 --> 00:05:32.000
There's 3, 4, 5
102 minus 197 will

114
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.895
become three goes here,

115
00:05:36.895 --> 00:05:41.565
four goes there,
five goes there.

116
00:05:41.565 --> 00:05:48.510
Then the second
row 201 minus 197.

117
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.415
But this is really taking
the three by three filter,

118
00:05:51.415 --> 00:05:53.650
and mirroring it both on

119
00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.080
the vertical and the
horizontal axis.

120
00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.970
Then it was this flipped matrix

121
00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:03.310
that you would then
copy over here.

122
00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.365
To compute the output,

123
00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:09.830
you would take 2*7 and so on.

124
00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.080
You actually multiply
out the elements of

125
00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.760
this filter matrix in order

126
00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.695
to compute the upper left-hand,

127
00:06:17.695 --> 00:06:25.295
most elements of the four
by four output as follows.

128
00:06:25.295 --> 00:06:28.570
Then you take those nine
numbers and your shift them

129
00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:31.880
over by one, and so on.

130
00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.505
The way we've defined

131
00:06:33.505 --> 00:06:35.710
the convolution operation
in these videos

132
00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:39.400
is that we've skipped
this mirroring operation.

133
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.085
Technically what
we're actually doing?

134
00:06:42.085 --> 00:06:43.510
Really the operation we've

135
00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:45.670
been using for the
last few videos

136
00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:50.130
is sometimes cross-correlation
instead of convolution.

137
00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.645
That in the deep
learning literature,

138
00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:54.370
by convention, we just call

139
00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.170
this the convolution operation.

140
00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.940
Just to summarize,
by convention,

141
00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.160
in machine learning,
we usually do

142
00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.710
not bother with this
flipping operation.

143
00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:08.255
Technically this operation is

144
00:07:08.255 --> 00:07:10.445
maybe better called
cross-correlation.

145
00:07:10.445 --> 00:07:12.620
But most of the deep
learning literature just

146
00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.610
causes the convolution operator.

147
00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:18.020
I'm going to use that convention

148
00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:19.400
in these videos as well,

149
00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:24.185
and if you read a lot of the
machine learning literature,

150
00:07:24.185 --> 00:07:26.570
you find most people
would just call this,

151
00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:28.430
the convolution operator without

152
00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:31.230
bothering to use these flips.

153
00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.590
It turns out that in

154
00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:36.995
signal processing or in certain
branches of mathematics,

155
00:07:36.995 --> 00:07:39.620
doing the flipping
in the definition of

156
00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:43.370
convolution causes
convolution operator,

157
00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:45.005
to enjoy this property that

158
00:07:45.005 --> 00:07:47.475
A convolve with B,
convolve with C,

159
00:07:47.475 --> 00:07:49.935
is equal to A convolve with B,

160
00:07:49.935 --> 00:07:51.890
convolve with C. And this is

161
00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:54.560
called associativity
in mathematics.

162
00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.680
This is nice for some signal
processing applications.

163
00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.195
But for deep neural networks,

164
00:07:59.195 --> 00:08:00.430
it really doesn't matter,

165
00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:03.890
and so omitting this
double mirroring operation

166
00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:05.980
just simplifies the code,

167
00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:10.060
and mixing neural
network just as well.

168
00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:14.690
By convention, most of us
just call this convolution.

169
00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.850
Even though, the
mathematicians prefer to

170
00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:19.800
call this cross-correlation
sometimes.

171
00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:24.140
But this should not affect
anything you have to

172
00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:26.930
implement in their
permanent exercises,

173
00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:29.390
and should not
affect your ability

174
00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:33.540
to read and understand the
deep learning literature.

175
00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.220
You've now seen how to
carry out convolutions,

176
00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:38.690
and you've seen how to use

